select m.id, 1, $L$What a Test Should Assert$L$, $L$17 minute read$L$, $L$An assertion is the whole point of a test — everything before it just sets the stage. This lesson is about aiming that assertion at behaviour and user-visible outcomes rather than at the incidental details of how the app is built.$L$, array[$L$Distinguish an outcome-focused assertion from an implementation-focused one$L$, $L$Explain what makes an assertion valuable enough to defend a behaviour$L$, $L$Rewrite a test so its assertion would go red if the feature genuinely broke$L$], $L$## The assertion is the test
Every line before the assertion is arrangement and action. The assertion is where the test earns its keep: it is the sentence that says "this is what must be true, or the product is wrong". If that sentence is weak, nothing else in the test matters — you can drive the app perfectly and still learn nothing.

## Assert behaviour, not implementation
Aim assertions at what a user would notice and care about:

- **Outcomes** — the confirmation message appeared, the basket total updated, the URL changed, the record now shows in the list.
- **Behaviour** — the disabled button became enabled once the form was valid; the error text explained what went wrong.

Avoid assertions coupled to how the app happens to be built today:

- A specific CSS class name, DOM depth, or internal component structure.
- A network call firing (the call is a means; the outcome is the end).
- An exact pixel position or a framework-generated id.

Implementation details change constantly during healthy refactoring. An assertion tied to them breaks when nothing is actually wrong — and, worse, stays green when the *behaviour* breaks but the class name survives.

## What makes an assertion valuable
A valuable assertion has one property above all: **it fails when, and only when, the behaviour it protects is broken.** Ask of every assertion, "if this feature silently stopped working, would this line go red?" If the answer is no, the assertion is decoration. If the answer is "it goes red even when the feature is fine, just restyled", the assertion is brittle. You want the narrow band in between: sensitive to real breakage, indifferent to cosmetic change.

## A green test is not automatically a good test
This is the message the whole course keeps returning to. Automation exists to give timely, useful evidence about product quality and risk. A test that passes while asserting nothing meaningful gives false evidence — it is worse than no test, because it buys unearned confidence. The green tick is only as trustworthy as the assertion behind it.

## Key takeaway
Point every assertion at a user-visible outcome or behaviour and ask "would this go red if the feature broke?"; an assertion that survives real breakage is not protecting anyone, however green it looks.$L$, $L$Two tests for the same login flow — one asserts an implementation detail, the other an outcome:

~~~ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

// Weak: asserts a network call fired and an element exists, not that login worked.
test('user logs in (implementation-focused)', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('priya@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('correct-horse');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  // The dashboard container renders for logged-out users too — this proves little.
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-shell')).toBeVisible();
});

// Strong: asserts the user-visible outcome of a successful login.
test('a valid user reaches their dashboard', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('priya@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('correct-horse');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();

  await expect(page).toHaveURL('/dashboard');
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Welcome back, Priya' })).toBeVisible();
});
~~~

The first test can pass even when authentication is broken, because the shell element is not unique to a logged-in user. The second asserts what a real user would experience — they land on the dashboard and are greeted by name. Break login and only the second test goes red.$L$, $L$- Asserting that an element is present when presence is not evidence the feature worked (containers often render regardless)
- Coupling assertions to CSS classes or DOM structure that a restyle will change without any behaviour changing
- Asserting a network request fired instead of asserting the outcome that request was supposed to produce
- Ending a test on the action ("the button was clicked") rather than on the result of the action$L$, $L$On a delivery team, make "what does this assert, and would it fail if the feature broke?" a standard review question. The cheapest way to expose a weak assertion is to imagine the feature quietly regressing and trace whether any line turns red. If none would, the test is a green light wired to nothing — and everyone downstream is trusting it. Reviewers who ask that one question catch more useless tests than any linter.$L$, $L$Take one of your existing UI tests and write down, in a sentence, exactly what its final assertion protects. Then answer honestly: if that feature silently broke, would the test go red? If not, rewrite the assertion to target the user-visible outcome. Deliverable: the before/after assertion plus your one-sentence statement of what the new assertion defends.$L$, $L$Think of a test you trust. If a developer refactored the component's internals without changing its behaviour, would your test survive? If a developer broke the behaviour but kept the markup, would it fail? What do your answers tell you about where your assertions are aimed?$L$, $L$What single question best exposes a weak assertion? (Answer: "If this feature silently broke, would this assertion go red?")$L$, array[$L$I can tell an outcome-focused assertion from an implementation-focused one$L$, $L$I can state, for any assertion, what behaviour it protects$L$, $L$I write assertions that fail on real breakage and survive cosmetic change$L$]
