select m.id, 5, $L$Tests as Documentation$L$, $L$16 minute read$L$, $L$The next engineer to touch this feature will read your tests before they read the code. Written well, a test suite is the most honest specification a team has — it describes what the system does and stays true because it runs. This lesson is about writing tests that communicate intent.$L$, array[$L$Name and structure tests so they describe behaviour to a future reader$L$, $L$Write test bodies that read as intent rather than as mechanics$L$, $L$Explain why executable tests are more trustworthy documentation than prose$L$], $L$## Tests are read more than they are written
A test is written once and read many times — during reviews, when a related feature changes, when it fails at 4pm before a release. Its first job is to *communicate*: what behaviour does this protect, and what does the feature promise? Code comments drift out of date silently; a test that lies gets caught, because it runs. That makes a well-written suite the most trustworthy documentation a team owns.

## Names that describe behaviour
The test name is the headline. Write it as a statement about behaviour a stakeholder could read:

- Not `test('login')` but `test('a valid user is taken to their dashboard')`.
- Not `test('quantity')` but `test('rejects a quantity above the maximum with a clear message')`.

A good name completes the sentence "it verifies that…". Read down a list of such names and you have a plain-English specification of the feature. Read a list of `test1`, `login`, `checkTable` and you have nothing.

## Structure that reads as intent
Inside the test, favour readability over cleverness:

- Group with `test.describe('Checkout', ...)` so the report reads as a feature outline.
- Keep the Arrange-Act-Assert sections visually distinct.
- Prefer user-facing locators — `getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' })` reads like an instruction; `page.locator('.btn-x7')` reads like noise.
- Extract fiddly setup into a well-named helper or fixture so the test body shows *what* happens, not *how* it is wired.

## Let the assertions state the promise
Because assertions are the specification (Lesson 1), they should read like promises the feature makes: `toHaveText('Payment successful')`, `toHaveURL('/dashboard')`. A reader should be able to skim only the assertions and know what the feature guarantees.

## Documentation that cannot go stale
Prose docs and code comments describe intent but are not checked against reality, so they rot. A test describes intent *and* is executed every run — if the behaviour changes and the test is not updated, it fails. That coupling is why "the tests are the spec" is more than a slogan: it is the one form of documentation the build refuses to let you falsify.

## Key takeaway
Write test names as behaviour statements and bodies as intent, so a reader learns what the feature does from the suite alone; because tests run, they are the one specification that cannot quietly drift out of date — but only if you name and structure them for the human who reads them next.$L$, $L$The same test written as mechanics, then as documentation:

~~~ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

// Reads as mechanics — the name and body tell a future reader almost nothing.
test('test2', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.locator('#e').fill('priya@example.com');
  await page.locator('#p').fill('correct-horse');
  await page.locator('.sbtn').click();
  await expect(page.locator('.dash h1')).toBeVisible();
});
~~~

~~~ts
// Reads as documentation — a stakeholder could understand what it guarantees.
test.describe('Login', () => {
  test('a valid user is greeted on their dashboard', async ({ page }) => {
    // Arrange
    await page.goto('/login');

    // Act
    await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('priya@example.com');
    await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('correct-horse');
    await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();

    // Assert
    await expect(page).toHaveURL('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Welcome back, Priya' })).toBeVisible();
  });
});
~~~

The behaviour is identical; the second version teaches the reader what login promises. The describe block, the behavioural name, the labelled sections and the user-facing locators all carry meaning the first version threw away.$L$, $L$- Naming tests after implementation or sequence (`test1`, `clickFlow`) instead of the behaviour they verify
- Filling test bodies with cryptic CSS locators that read as noise to the next engineer
- Relying on out-of-line comments to explain a test that clearer names and locators would make self-evident
- Writing the name for yourself today rather than for the teammate debugging it under pressure next quarter$L$, $L$On a delivery team, run `npx playwright test --list` and read the test titles as if they were the feature's specification. If a product owner could not follow what the system does from that list, the names are too technical. Some teams deliberately review the list of test names in refinement as a lightweight acceptance-criteria check — the suite doubles as living documentation only if the names are written for humans, so it is worth treating naming as part of the work, not an afterthought.$L$, $L$Take a spec file with vague test names and rewrite every name as a behaviour statement that completes "it verifies that…". Group related tests under a `test.describe`, and replace at least three cryptic locators with user-facing ones. Deliverable: the before/after list of test names and a note on whether the file now reads as a specification of the feature.$L$, $L$If a new engineer joined tomorrow and read only your test names for a feature, could they describe what it does and what rules it enforces? If not, what is the documentation gap — the code's, or the tests'?$L$, $L$Why are executable tests more trustworthy documentation than code comments? (Answer: tests run every build, so if the behaviour changes without the test being updated the test fails — comments can silently go stale because nothing checks them)$L$, array[$L$I can name a test as a behaviour statement a stakeholder could read$L$, $L$I can structure a test body so it reads as intent, not mechanics$L$, $L$I can explain why running tests are documentation that cannot quietly rot$L$]
